import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>10.1c — Recursive Dungeon Crawler: Explore All Rooms</h1>

<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Expand Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h3>Build: Dungeon Explorer</h3>
        <p>Dungeons are a natural fit for recursion. A room has doors to other rooms, which have doors to more rooms... recursion explores them all without needing to know the layout in advance.</p>
        <p>Create a class called <code>DungeonExplorer</code> with this dungeon represented as a 2D char array:</p>
        <pre>char[][] dungeon = {
    {'#', '#', '#', '#', '#'},
    {'#', '.', '.', '.', '#'},
    {'#', '.', '.', '.', '#'},
    {'#', '.', '.', '.', '#'},
    {'#', '#', '#', '#', '#'}
};</pre>
        <ol>
            <li><code>void explore(int row, int col, boolean[][] visited)</code>: mark the current tile visited. Then recursively explore all 4 adjacent tiles that are floor ('.') and not yet visited.</li>
            <li><code>int countRooms()</code>: start from any floor tile, call explore, and return the number of tiles visited.</li>
            <li>Add a treasure room: change one tile to 'T'. Your explore method should print a message when it finds treasure.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h3>Base Case Check</h3>
        <p>What are ALL the base cases in your explore method? List them. Missing even one causes infinite recursion or array bounds errors.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/recursion-basics.html">Recursion</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-08/2d-array-basics.html">2D Arrays</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
